\title{mcplite: Lightweight stdio MCP server for R}
\description{
Expose R functions as tools for a local Model Context Protocol (MCP) client.
Define tools, save them in a script ending with \code{\link[=mcp_server]{mcp_server()}}, and configure
the client to launch that script with \code{Rscript --vanilla} and an absolute
script path. The server blocks until standard input closes.
}
\section{Define tools and return results}{

Use \code{mcplite::tool()} with the \link{tool-types} helpers to describe arguments.
Tools remain directly callable R functions. Return ordinary R values for
text conversion, or \code{\link[=tool_result]{tool_result()}} with \code{\link[=content_text]{content_text()}} and other
\link{content-blocks} constructors for structured or rich output. See
\link{mcplite-results} for exact return-value mappings and payload JSON options.

Schemas do not validate values or enforce permissions. Tools own domain
validation, authorization, safe side effects, and output limits. Keep stdout
exclusively for MCP protocol messages; use stderr for diagnostics.
}

\section{Scope and next steps}{

\code{mcplite} is tools-only and stdio-only; embedded resources and resource links
in results do not provide resource endpoints. See \code{\link[=mcp_server]{mcp_server()}} for server
use, supported protocol versions, and optional tracing. Compatible
\code{ellmer::tool()} objects are accepted, but ellmer is not required.

Website guides: \href{https://tosidata.github.io/mcplite/articles/mcplite.html}{Get started},
\href{https://tosidata.github.io/mcplite/articles/writing-tools.html}{Write tools},
and \href{https://tosidata.github.io/mcplite/articles/returning-results.html}{Return results}.
